>
  <p class="lead"><strong>No, not by the legal definition.</strong> In the United States, a frozen dessert needs at least 10% milkfat to be sold as "ice cream." Dairy Queen says its soft serve has about 5% butterfat, so DQ calls it soft serve. It is still a real dairy product made from milk, just lighter than ice cream.</p>

  <h2 id="the-rule">The 10% milkfat rule</h2>
  <p>The FDA has a "standard of identity" for ice cream, written in federal regulation 21 CFR 135.110. To use the name, a product must contain at least 10% milkfat and meet minimum milk-solids levels. Anything below that has to use another name, such as soft serve, frozen dairy dessert or reduced-fat ice cream.</p>
  <p>That is why you will see "soft serve," "Blizzard Treat" and "frozen treats" on DQ menus, not "ice cream." It is a labeling rule, not a sign that something fake is going on.</p>

  <h2 id="comparison">Dairy Queen soft serve vs. ice cream</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="size-table">
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>DQ soft serve</th><th>Regular ice cream</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Milkfat</strong></td><td>About 5%</td><td>At least 10%; premium brands often 14% or more</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Texture</strong></td><td>Soft, light and smooth, served straight from the machine</td><td>Firm and dense, scooped from a freezer</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Air</strong></td><td>More air whipped in, which makes it lighter</td><td>Less air, so it feels heavier</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Serving temperature</strong></td><td>A little warmer than hard ice cream, so it tastes sweeter and creamier right away</td><td>Colder; flavors come through as it softens</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ingredients">What is in DQ soft serve?</h2>
  <p>The main ingredients are milk, milkfat, sugar, corn syrup and whey. Small amounts of stabilizers such as guar gum and carrageenan keep it smooth and stop ice crystals from forming. There is no lard or "plastic" in it; those are long-running internet myths. If you have allergies, see our <a href="/blog/dq-gluten-free-allergen-guide">Dairy Queen allergen guide</a> and DQ’s own ingredient information.</p>

  <h2 id="upside-down">Why a Blizzard can be held upside down</h2>
  <p>Soft serve is thick enough to hold its shape, and blending in cookies or candy makes it thicker still. That is why staff can flip a <a href="/categories/blizzard-treats">Blizzard</a> upside down at the window. Fruit-heavy flavors are the most likely to fail the flip, because fruit adds water.</p>

  <h2 id="what-to-order">Where to try it</h2>
  <p>To taste DQ soft serve on its own, get a <a href="/items/vanilla-soft-serve-cone">vanilla cone</a> or a <a href="/items/chocolate-dipped-cone">dipped cone</a>. For more, see the <a href="/categories/classic-treats">cones and sundaes menu</a> or our ranking of the <a href="/blog/best-blizzard-flavors-ranked">best Blizzard flavors</a>.</p>
